    84	**Proof.** By translation, set beta_0=0. Let H_n=sum_{i<=n}1/i. The right and left derivatives at zero are
    85	
    86	D_n^+ = sum_i [1{epsilon_i=0}+1{epsilon_i=-1}-1{epsilon_i=1}]/i,
    87	D_n^- = sum_i [-1{epsilon_i=0}+1{epsilon_i=-1}-1{epsilon_i=1}]/i.
    88	
    89	Their expectations are H_n/2 and -H_n/2. Each centered series converges almost surely because its independent, centered summands have summable variances, bounded by sum i^{-2}. One elementary justification is that the partial sums form an L^2-bounded martingale, hence converge almost surely. Thus D_n^+->+infinity and D_n^-->-infinity almost surely. Eventually D_n^-<0<D_n^+. For a convex function this makes zero the unique global minimizer: its secant slope to any positive point is at least D_n^+>0, and its secant slope from any negative point to zero is at most D_n^-<0. Finally sum i^{-2}<1+integral_1^infinity t^{-2}dt=2. QED.
    90	
    91	### Theorem 4B (continuous bounded errors and a flat-bottom loss)
    92	
    93	Take x_i=1/i, errors uniform on [-1,1], and rho(r)=(|r|-1)_+, where a_+=max(a,0). Every minimizing selection is strongly consistent for every beta_0, although the same S_n remains bounded.
    94	
    95	**Proof.** At beta_0 every loss is zero. Writing h=b-beta_0, the entire minimizing set is exactly the compact interval
    96	
    97	[L_n,U_n],  L_n=max_{i<=n} i(epsilon_i-1),  U_n=min_{i<=n} i(epsilon_i+1).
    98	
    99	Here L_n<=0<=U_n. For any fixed delta>0 and all sufficiently large i,
   100	
   101	P(i(epsilon_i+1)<delta)=delta/(2i).
   102	
   103	These independent events have divergent probability sum, so the second Borel-Cantelli lemma gives U_n->0 almost surely. Applying the same argument to 1-epsilon_i gives L_n->0. A countable intersection over positive rational delta proves that the full minimizing interval shrinks to {0}. QED.
   104	
   105	Both examples rule out an unqualified claim that lambda_min(S_n)->infinity is necessary for every nondegenerate error law and every convex rho. They do not refute its necessity for Gaussian-error least squares or for appropriately regular identifiable location experiments. Neither example refutes duplication: consistency is preserved, even strongly under S. For 4A, the pooled right derivative has mean H_n and the pooled left derivative has mean -H_n. Each is a sum of 2n independent terms at a fixed n, with ranges of length 2/i occurring twice. Hoeffding's inequality bounds either wrong-sign probability by exp(-H_n^2/(4 S_n))<=exp(-H_n^2/8). Since H_n>=log(n+1), these bounds are summable; the first Borel-Cantelli lemma gives eventual unique recovery at zero. For 4B, every pooled loss remains nonnegative and zero at the truth, and the pooled minimizing interval is contained in the original shrinking interval. For R, the corresponding claims also follow from the two fixed streams.
   106	
   107	## 5. A genuine failure under triangular designs, with no arbitrary selection trick
   108	
   109	### Theorem 5
   110	
   111	There is a fixed finite coercive convex loss, bounded nondegenerate i.i.d. errors, p=4, and deterministic triangular designs for which **every original minimizing selection is strongly consistent**, but **every replicated minimizing selection is weakly inconsistent**. This is not a counterexample for a single nested design sequence.
   112	
   113	**Construction.** Let rho(r)=10|r|+(r-1)_+, errors be independent Rademacher variables, and beta_0=0. Fix c=1/10. At stage m use n_m=6m+2 rows and coordinates
   114	
   115	z=(u_1,u_2,t,v)=(m beta_1,m beta_2,m beta_3,beta_4).
   116	
   117	The first two predictions are u_1 and u_2. For each of the following six linear forms L_r(z), include m rows whose prediction is -(2c/m)L_r(z):
   118	
   119	L_1=2(u_1-t), L_2=2(-u_1-t),
   120	L_3=2(u_2+v-t), L_4=2(-u_2+v-t),
   121	L_5=t/2, L_6=(t-v)/2.
   122	
   123	This specifies ordinary deterministic design vectors in the beta coordinates. Couple errors by using two fixed high-row errors e_1,e_2 and six independent infinite Rademacher streams for the six groups. All errors within any stage are i.i.d. Use a second independent copy of these eight streams for the repeated experiment. Sizes between n_m and n_{m+1} may be defined by the stage-m design plus zero rows; their extra losses do not affect minimizers.
   124	
   125	Define G(z)=sum_{r=1}^6 (L_r(z))_+.
   126	
   127	**Lemma 5.1 (the exact geometry).** For fixed u_1,u_2, G has a unique minimizing pair
   128	
   129	t=|u_1|, v=|u_1|-|u_2|,
   130	
   131	and its minimum is (|u_1|+|u_2|)/2. Moreover G is coercive on R^4.
   132	
   133	**Proof.** Fix also v and put M=max(|u_1|,|u_2|+v). Below t=M at least one of the four first positive parts is active, contributing slope -2 with respect to t. The last two terms together contribute slope at most +1; all other active constraint terms contribute nonpositive slopes. Thus G strictly decreases up to M. At t>=M, all constraint penalties vanish, t>=0, and t-v>=0, giving G=t-v/2, strictly increasing in t. Its unique minimizing t is therefore M. The remaining expression max(a,b+v)-v/2, where a=|u_1| and b=|u_2|, strictly decreases until v=a-b and strictly increases afterwards. This proves the formula and uniqueness. G is continuous, positively homogeneous, nonnegative, and zero only at z=0 by the formula and uniqueness. Its minimum on the unit sphere is positive, which proves coercivity. QED.
   134	
   135	**Lemma 5.2 (local uniform almost-sure limits).** Subtract the low-row objectives' values at z=0. On every compact set of z, the original objectives converge uniformly almost surely to
   136	
   137	Q(z)=rho(e_1-u_1)+rho(e_2-u_2)+cG(z).
   138	
   139	The doubled objectives converge similarly to
   140	
   141	Q_2(z)=sum_{a=1}^2 [rho(e_a-u_a)+rho(e_a'-u_a)]+2cG(z).
   142	
   143	**Proof.** Put d=2c/m. On a fixed compact set, |d L_r|<1 eventually. For one row with prediction -d L and error e,
   144	
   145	rho(e+d L)-rho(e)=10 e d L + 1{e=1} d(L)_+.
   146	
   147	This is an exact identity in that region. For each group its sum is
   148	
   149	20c (m^{-1}sum e_i)L + 2c(m^{-1}sum 1{e_i=1})(L)_+.
   150	
   151	The strong law sends the two coefficients to 0 and c. There are only six groups and finitely many linear forms, so convergence is uniform on every compact set, almost surely. A countable sequence of balls makes this simultaneous on all compacts. The duplicate proof is identical. QED.
   152	
   153	**Minimizers and limits.** For fixed u_1,u_2, Lemma 5.1 uniquely determines t,v in both limit objectives. The reduced original objective is
   154	
   155	sum_{a=1}^2 [rho(e_a-u_a)+(c/2)|u_a|].
   156	
   157	Since rho(r)>=10|r| and rho(0)=0, the reverse triangle inequality shows that replacing u_a=e_a by any different value increases this expression by at least (10-c/2)|u_a-e_a|. Thus Q has the unique minimizer
   158	
   159	(e_1,e_2,1,0).
   160	
   161	For Q_2 the reduced coordinate objective is
   162	
   163	rho(e-u)+rho(e'-u)+c|u|.
   164	
   165	If e=e', its unique minimizer is u=e by the same inequality. If e=-e', then for -1<=u<=1 its first two terms equal 20+(-u)_+. Adding c|u| makes the unique minimum u=0; convexity excludes minima outside this interval. Write s_a=e_a if e_a=e_a', and s_a=0 otherwise. The unique minimizer of Q_2 is
   166	
   167	(s_1,s_2,|s_1|,|s_1|-|s_2|).
   168	
   169	For completeness, local uniform convergence of convex functions to a coercive continuous function with a unique minimizer forces every minimizer to converge to that point. Choose a sphere centered at the limiting minimizer on which the limiting objective exceeds its minimum by a positive amount. Uniform convergence on that sphere and at its center gives the same strict inequality for all sufficiently large finite objectives. If one had a minimizer outside the sphere, convexity along the segment from the center to it would contradict the sphere inequality. Compactness then puts all minimizers in any prescribed neighborhood by uniform convergence and the uniqueness gap. In the present construction every finite objective has minimizers: its design has rank four, and rho is coercive, so its objective is coercive.
   170	
   171	Consequently all original beta estimates converge almost surely to zero: their first three coordinates are bounded z coordinates divided by m, and their fourth coordinate tends to zero. In the doubled experiment the fourth coordinate converges almost surely to |s_1|-|s_2|, which has probabilities 1/4,1/2,1/4 at -1,0,+1. In particular,
   172	
   173	lim_m P(|beta_hat_{2n_m,4}|>1/2)=1/2.
   174	
   175	There is no tie-breaking pathology: every minimizing selection has the asserted limit. QED.
   176	
   177	**Why this does not settle the nested problem.** The first two rows change with m, and the fourth components of low rows in groups 3, 4, and 6 change with m. One cannot reinterpret this array as prefixes of a fixed x_i sequence. Attempts to concatenate stages leave old weak-direction penalties in the objective and destroy the argument. This obstruction has not been overcome.
   178	
   179	## 6. A literal arbitrary-selection version is false, but for an uninteresting reason
   180	
   181	### Theorem 6 (selection diagnostic, not a resolution of the intended conjecture)
   182	
   183	Use p=1, beta_0=0, x_i=2^{-i}, errors equal to -1/4 or +1/4 independently, and rho(r)=(|r|-1)_+. Define one fixed measurable rule on a finite scalar dataset as follows: among its minimizers, choose the point closest to zero if all nonzero design rows are distinct; otherwise choose the largest minimizer. For other datasets where a largest minimizer does not exist, use the closest-to-zero selection; this fallback is irrelevant here.
   184	
   185	Every original estimate is exactly zero, but every duplicated-design estimate is at least 3/2. Both weak and strong implications fail for this rule.
   186	
   187	**Proof.** The minimizing interval is the intersection of [2^i(epsilon_i-1),2^i(epsilon_i+1)]. All these intervals contain [-3/2,3/2]. The first row's interval is contained in [-5/2,5/2], while all later rows' intervals contain [-3,3], so the first row determines the original interval. The original designs are distinct, hence the rule chooses zero. In the duplicated design there are two first-row intervals and all later intervals are wider; their intersection still contains [-3/2,3/2] and has a largest point at least 3/2. The fixed rule chooses that point. These choices are measurable (finite maxima, minima, and a deterministic test on the design). QED.
   188	
   189	This estimator is intentionally non-equivariant and changes its tie preference when rows repeat. Its consistency is only asserted at beta_0=0. It explains why an unspecified argmin cannot support a universal theorem. It is not evidence against a uniqueness-based or stable-selection version, and it does not invalidate Theorem 1's stated hypotheses.
   190	
   191	## 7. A necessary-information lemma for the actual nested problem
   192	
   193	### Theorem 7 (regular location errors and equivariant estimation)
   194	
   195	Suppose the errors have a density f on R, q=sqrt(f) belongs to the Sobolev space H^1(R), and J=4 integral |q'(x)|^2 dx is finite. Let x_i be one fixed deterministic infinite design. If a translation-equivariant estimator is weakly consistent at one parameter value, then lambda_min(S_n)->infinity. Translation equivariance means T_n(y+X_n h)=T_n(y)+h for every parameter shift h. Unique M-minimizers automatically have this property wherever they exist.
   196	
   197	This is a necessity theorem, not a sufficiency theorem for an arbitrary convex rho.
   198	
   199	**Proof.** Equivariance makes the estimation-error distribution independent of beta_0, so consistency holds at every parameter value. Suppose lambda_min(S_n) does not diverge. Since S_n increases in the positive-semidefinite order, these smallest eigenvalues are bounded by a constant C. Choose unit eigenvectors along a subsequence converging to a unit vector u. For each fixed m and all later n, u_n'S_m u_n<=u_n'S_n u_n<=C. Passing to the subsequential limit gives u'S_m u<=C for every m. Therefore sum_i (x_i'u)^2<=C.
   200	
   201	For any scalar h, the H^1 translation bound gives
   202	
   203	||q(.-h)-q||_2 <= |h| ||q'||_2.
   204	
   205	Indeed, write the difference as an integral of translates of q' and apply the integral triangle inequality; the standard absolutely continuous representative of an H^1 function justifies this identity. Its Hellinger affinity with its translate is
   206	
   207	A(h)=integral q(x)q(x-h)dx
   208	    =1-(1/2)||q(.-h)-q||_2^2
   209	    >=1-J h^2/8.
   210	
   211	Choose a nonzero constant a sufficiently small that J a^2 C/8<=1/2. If C=0 no smallness is needed: every x_i'u=0 and the two experiments are identical. Put h_i=a x_i'u. The affinity between the first n observations at beta_0 and at beta_0+a u is the product A_n=product_{i<=n}A(h_i). Since all J h_i^2/8<=1/2 and log(1-z)>=-2z on [0,1/2],
   212	
   213	A_n >= exp[-(J/4) sum_{i<=n}h_i^2] >= exp(-J a^2 C/4)>0.
   214	
   215	For any two densities with affinity A, Cauchy-Schwarz applied to |sqrt(p)-sqrt(q)|(sqrt(p)+sqrt(q)) gives total variation at most sqrt(1-A^2). Thus these two n-sample experiments have total variation bounded uniformly strictly below one. But consistency at both parameters gives a contradiction: the event that T_n is within |a|/3 of beta_0 has probability tending to one at beta_0 and to zero at beta_0+a u. Its probability difference tends to one, which total variation forbids. QED.
   216	
   217	For Gaussian errors of variance sigma^2, J=1/sigma^2. The lemma also covers other finite-location-information densities satisfying the displayed H^1 condition; it does not cover atomic laws or the uniform-density support boundary in Theorems 4A/4B. It closes the *necessary design condition* for equivariant estimators in this regular class. Applying a suitable independently established M-consistency sufficiency theorem would then give weak transfer because duplication replaces S_n by 2S_n. A general sufficiency theorem for every finite convex coercive rho has not been established here, and the truncated 1993 preview is not used to assume one.
   218	
   219	## 8. What has not been proved
   220	
   221	No proof or non-selection counterexample is supplied for weak preservation under one fixed infinite deterministic design with p>=2 and unique original/pooled minimizers. Nor is the analogous strong R statement established in those dimensions outside the least-squares or other sufficient-condition regimes. The book additionally says that the error law satisfies unspecified conditions; those conditions must be made explicit before attributing any counterexample to the author's intended regularity class.

